{
  "dom_id": "step-q20681",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Consider a triangle $△ABC$, where the measure of an exterior angle at $C$ is equal to ${127}^{∘}$ and $m∠B={73}^{∘}.$ What is the measure of the angle $A?$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's start by drawing the corresponding triangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191822/assets/0729bbdc2bec04cee7be1f0be31a44648b05a2536f7e17002d1dab6e2f3500c6.png)\n\n*Method 1*\n\nLet's start by finding the value of the missing interior angle at $C.$ We use the fact that the interior and exterior angles are supplementary:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{127}^{∘}+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & ={180}^{∘}-{127}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & ={53}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $∠A$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A+{73}^{∘}+{53}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={180}^{∘}-{126}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={54}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n*Method 2*\n\nWe could rewrite the equation $m∠A+m∠B+m∠C={180}^{∘}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠C}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at }C}\\,\\,\\,=m∠A+m∠B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we can solve for $m∠A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠C}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at }C}\\,\\,\\, & =m∠A+m∠B \\\\ {127}^{∘} & =m∠A+{73}^{∘} \\\\ {127}^{∘}-{73}^{∘} & =m∠A \\\\ {54}^{∘} & =m∠A\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
